      {
        "id": 110277,
        "name": "phantaskippy",
        "username": "phantaskippy",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/{size}/1897_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-08-06T02:03:48.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Foote\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/48/1877_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Foote:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>T he most current Order of Operations ruling actually does supersede those older rulings. Static effects were a forum created term to try and define these interactions before Christopher did away with all of that with the OoO ruling.&nbsp;Any static effect without a defined timing (like damage type modifiers) are all simultaneous with eachother.&nbsp;Anything that's not a start or end of turn effect are no longer bound by \"whichever came out first\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Except that \"Static Effect\" has been used by Admins multiple times when making rulings. &nbsp;Christopher's ruling on OoO handled simultaneous effects. &nbsp;The old ruling that Static effects don't have timing isn't superceeded by that because the whole point of static effects not having timing is that they weren't under the OoO to begin with.</p>\n<p>We also already have an exception to the OoO ruling with Informant. &nbsp;While all of this is nice and clean, what it isn't is official. &nbsp;Because there hasn't been a direct ruling that did away with the ruling that some effects don't have timing, the application of the new OoO to static effects is questionable.</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
