      {
        "id": 110279,
        "name": "Foote",
        "username": "Foote",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-08-06T13:45:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"phantaskippy\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/48/1897_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> phantaskippy:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Except that \"Static Effect\" has been used by Admins multiple times when making rulings. &nbsp;Christopher's ruling on OoO handled simultaneous effects. &nbsp;The old ruling that Static effects don't have timing isn't superceeded by that because the whole point of static effects not having timing is that they weren't under the OoO to begin with.We also already have an exception to the OoO ruling with Informant. &nbsp;While all of this is nice and clean, what it isn't is official. &nbsp;Because there hasn't been a direct ruling that did away with the ruling that some effects don't have timing, the application of the new OoO to static effects is questionable.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<ol>\n<li>\"Static Effects\" were a forum constructed term in the pre OoO days to try and explain how interactions worked and resolved when timing clauses were absent. It's not a official term or rule and never has been. Is it in the glossary? Nope.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All of those old \"static effects\" are now simultaneous with eachother. They are assumed to trigger at the same time. That alone puts it under the heading of the OoO. Without distinct timing clauses, you can't rightfully argue they trigger consecutivy.</p>\n<p>The OoO was not just about Simultaneous effects either. The heart of the ruling was to do away with \"what card came out first\" tracking, except for the specifically mentioned Start/End of turn tracking. And It specifically said at the end of the post that any other ambiguous situations are at the discretion of players choice. So even if \"static effects\" are not simultaneous, they fall under \"ambiguous\" due to the lack of timing clause. Either way, its OoO material.</p>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li>Informant actually does not break the OoO ruling because it actually doesn't fall under \"simultaneous\" effects at all. It is consecutive. The playing of a card happens before the text of that card activates. That was the informant ruling. So Informant plays the extra card (as it is the newest trigger), then you resolve the card the hero played.</li>\n</ol>",
        "post_number": 24,
        "post_type": 1,
